{
  "abstract": "The Hobart–Brouwer table lists the directed strongly regular graph parameters $(100,42,36,14,20)$ and $(100,47,41,24,20)$, and their complements $(100,57,51,34,30)$ and $(100,52,46,24,30)$, as unresolved. We construct dsrgs with all four. Let $V=\\mathbb F_5^2$ and let $K\\le\\mathrm{GL}(2,5)$ be a subgroup of order 8 containing $-I$. The group $G_K=V\\rtimes K$ acts on the 100 cosets of $\\{\\pm I\\}$, and an invariant digraph is given by the out-neighbourhood $T$ of one vertex. For three groups $K$ (two non-conjugate subgroups $C_4\\times C_2$ and a cyclic $C_8$) and both degrees we give $T$ explicitly. By the group-ring criterion of rStation paper 69, each graph is a dsrg exactly when $D^2=2(tH+\\lambda D+\\mu(G_K-H-D))$ in $\\mathbb Z[G_K]$. Two independent standard-library scripts, one in the group ring and one counting two-walks, check the six graphs and their complements. We prove that $G_K$ has a regular subgroup if and only if $K$ has a subgroup of order 4 avoiding $-I$; so the graphs for one $K$ are Cayley digraphs, and those for the other two are vertex-transitive but not Cayley. By computer, every graph has automorphism group $G_K$ of order 200 and is not isomorphic to its converse, and for each degree the three graphs and their converses are pairwise non-isomorphic, which gives at least six dsrgs for each parameter set. We also prove a character constraint for every coset dsrg whose point stabiliser has order 2: for each nontrivial irreducible character $\\chi$, $\\chi(D)=2(ra_\\chi+s(m_\\chi-a_\\chi))$ with integers $0\\le a_\\chi\\le m_\\chi$, where $r,s$ are the nontrivial eigenvalues and $m_\\chi$ is the dimension of the stabiliser-fixed space. Column orthogonality then fixes $|D\\cap C|$ for every conjugacy class $C$ up to a short list. With these constraints, a CP-SAT search over unions of orbitals found the graphs in 3 to 124 seconds; without them it gave no answer in 200 seconds on the non-Cayley actions.",
